<Project>
  <!-- Pin transitive System.Security.Cryptography.Xml to a patched version.
       Pulled in transitively via FSharp.Compiler.Service / FSharpLint.

       This pin has now moved twice, so record the chain: 9.0.0 was vulnerable
       (GHSA-37gx-xxp4-5rgx, high), which is why the pin existed at all; then
       10.0.9 itself became affected by a second round of high-severity
       advisories (GHSA-23rf-6693-g89p, -8q5v-6pqq-x66h, -cvvh-rhrc-wg4q,
       -g8r8-53c2-pm3f, -mmjf-rqrv-855v) whose ranges cover >=10.0.0,<=10.0.9.
       Patched in 10.0.10.

       Bump this rather than suppressing NU1903 — the warning is doing its job.
       Kept in step with FsHotWatch's identical pin; both consume the same
       FCS/FSharpLint dependency graph, so they go stale together.

       NOTE this pin cannot reach the vendored FSharpLint fork under
       paket-files/: that fork ships its own Directory.Build.props with no
       import-of-the-file-above, so MSBuild's first-match-wins walk stops there
       and it resolves the package through its own Directory.Packages.props.
       That was fixed by a push to michaelglass/FSharpLint
       (perf/two-phase-lint-api): the fork's own Directory.Packages.props is on
       10.0.10 as of fork commit 49c68e17, which paket.lock pins. Dropping the
       fork would remove the whole class: as a NuGet package rather than a
       vendored project, FSharpLint.Core stops carrying its own build config and
       falls back under this pin. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="System.Security.Cryptography.Xml" Version="10.0.10" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <!-- RefStamp: ref-stamp guard for local packs (satisfies fsprojlint's
       "local packs are ref-stamped" check). Build/test-inert (PrivateAssets=all). -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="RefStamp" Version="0.1.0-alpha.2" PrivateAssets="all" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
